"# Chapter 1 — What a library is (and why Motoi has no `module`)

A library is a bag of verbs with a name. That is the entire idea. In Motoi, though, you do not spell that bag out yourself with a `module` form, and you do not pull it in with an `import` — because Motoi loads its libraries *for you*. This chapter is about that.

A note before we start: this book was written across a family of Schemes, and some Schemes give you an explicit module system — a `(module ...)` form to declare a bag of verbs, an `(import ...)` form to pull one in. **Motoi is not one of those Schemes.** Motoi is *batteries-included*: its libraries are already loaded when the interpreter boots. So as you read, treat `module` and `import` the way you'd treat a foreign word — recognize it, know what it means in the other dialect, and know that in Motoi you simply call the verb.

Motoi's core is small. There is arithmetic. There are lists and vectors and hashmaps. There are records and closures. There is a reader that turns text into forms, and a printer that turns forms back into text. There are a few things for talking to time and the outside world. But sitting right next to that core, always loaded, are Motoi's standard libraries: SRFI-1 for lists, SRFI-13 for strings, SRFI-125 for hash tables, SRFI-27 for randomness — and Motoi's own runtime verbs. You never turn these on. They are on.

## 1.1 Your first library call — no ceremony

In a module-having Scheme, you would first declare a bag of verbs, then import it, then call from it. In Motoi you skip the first two steps entirely. The verbs are already here. You just call one:

```scheme
(string-append \"Hello, \" \"Alfred\" \".\")
;; ⇒ \"Hello, Alfred.\"
```

`string-append` is a library verb. There was no `(import ...)` line above it, and there did not need to be. This is the whole loop in Motoi: the libraries are loaded; you call the verbs. Everything in the rest of this book is elaboration of that one fact — and, where the book reaches for `module` or `import`, an honest note that those are *other* dialects' words, not Motoi's.

## 1.2 Why Motoi loads libraries for you

Some languages make you import because their base is tiny and every feature you don't import is weight you don't carry. That is a real design, and it is the design this book was originally written to describe. Motoi makes a different bargain: its standard library is fixed, known, and small enough to always carry. So rather than make every program open with a stack of import lines, Motoi just brings the standard library in and lets you start writing.

The payoff is simplicity. There is no import to forget, no import to get wrong, no version to pin at an import site, no collision to resolve. For a language meant to be picked up on day one — by beginners, by kids — \"the verbs are just there\" is the kinder deal.

That does not mean names never clash across the wider world of code; it means Motoi's *own* standard library is curated so its verbs live together peacefully. When you write your own helpers, you name them; when you call a library verb, it is already named for you (often with a slash, like `hash-table-ref/default` or `cortex/recall`).

## 1.3 What a library is not

Because *module* and *library* are words that have meant many things in many languages, let me clear the ground.

A library is not a class. Classes bundle data with methods. A library is a bag of verbs. If you want data-with-methods behavior, you use records for the data and call verbs on them.

A library is not a file you import. In Motoi there is no import step at all, so a library is not \"the thing at the top of your file.\" It is simply a set of verbs the runtime has already loaded.

A library is not the `(module ...)` form. That form belongs to other Schemes. Motoi has no `module` special form; typing `(module foo ...)` yields `error: unbound symbol: module`. Recognize the form, know it is not Motoi's.

## 1.4 The libraries Motoi loads for you

Motoi's standard is R7RS-small (the core) plus a named set of SRFI parts, all loaded at boot:

- **R7RS-small** — the core: arithmetic, `car`/`cdr`/`cons`, `list`, `if`, `define`, `lambda`, and the rest of the kernel.
- **SRFI-1** — list verbs: `fold`, `filter`, `delete-duplicates`, `iota`, `take`, and friends.
- **SRFI-13** — string verbs: `string-upcase`, `string-downcase`, `string-trim`, `string-join`, `string-split`.
- **SRFI-125** — hash tables: `make-hash-table`, `hash-table-ref/default`, and the rest.
- **SRFI-27** — randomness: `random`, `random-integer`.

You do not choose these at the top of a file. They are the meal already on the table.

## 1.5 A slightly larger example — using several libraries at once

Let me show a small program that leans on three libraries — strings, lists, and randomness — with no import lines anywhere:

```scheme
(define (heads-or-tails n)
  (if (< n 0.5) 'heads 'tails))

(define (flip)
  (heads-or-tails (random)))

(define (flip-run count)
  (let loop ((n count) (heads 0) (tails 0))
    (if (= n 0)
        (list :heads heads :tails tails)
        (case (flip)
          ((heads) (loop (- n 1) (+ heads 1) tails))
          ((tails) (loop (- n 1) heads (+ tails 1)))))))

(let ((result (flip-run 100)))
  (list (integer? (cadr result))
        (integer? (cadddr result))))
;; ⇒ (#t #t)
```

`random` came from SRFI-27, `list` and `<` and `case` from the core, and none of them needed announcing. Notice there is no `(module coin-flip ...)` wrapper and no `(export ...)` list — Motoi has neither of those forms. You write your `define`s and you call them.

## 1.6 There is no `motoi/base` module to inspect

In a module-having Scheme you might ask a library for its export list. Some Schemes even model the base language itself as a module you can query. Motoi does not. There is no `(module-verbs 'motoi/base)` to call — `module-verbs` is another dialect's introspection verb and yields `error: unbound symbol: module-verbs` in Motoi.

Instead of asking a `module` object what it exports, you just *use* the verbs, and if you reach for one that isn't loaded, Motoi tells you plainly with an `unbound symbol` error. The library's contents are discovered by calling, not by querying a module record.

## 1.7 The rest of this book — read through the dialect lens

Sixteen chapters and eight appendices follow, and they were written around a module system. Read them through Motoi's lens: wherever the book says *module* or *import* or *export*, hear \"in some other Scheme.\" The chapters that describe machinery Motoi lacks (versions, hashes, signatures, forking) are kept as honest dialect-boundary lessons — they teach you what *other* Schemes do and, by contrast, sharpen what Motoi does instead. Where a chapter's lesson maps onto something real in Motoi (composing small pieces, keeping helpers private with `define`, observing behavior with Cortex), the Motoi version is shown in runnable code.

## 1.8 The core stays small — but you never import to grow it

Motoi's core is small on purpose, and its standard libraries are loaded alongside it. What Motoi does *not* do is make you assemble a language out of imports before you can begin. There is no import ritual gating \"anything more interesting than arithmetic.\" You open a file and the whole standard library is already yours.

## 1.9 A worked example — three helpers cooperating

Here is a small program that greets a name, bids it farewell, and picks a random sparkle to punctuate. In the other dialect this would be three modules; in Motoi it is three `define`s and some library calls:

```scheme
(define (hello-in lang)
  (cond ((eq? lang :es) \"Hola\")
        ((eq? lang :ja) \"こんにちは\")
        (else \"Hello\")))

(define (goodbye-in lang)
  (cond ((eq? lang :es) \"Adiós\")
        ((eq? lang :ja) \"さようなら\")
        (else \"Goodbye\")))

(define sparkles #(\"*\" \"~\" \".\" \"o\"))

(define (sparkle)
  (vector-ref sparkles (random-integer (vector-length sparkles))))

(define (greet-and-part name lang)
  (list (string-append (hello-in lang) \", \" name \" \" (sparkle))
        (string-append (goodbye-in lang) \", \" name \" \" (sparkle))))

(length (greet-and-part \"Alfred\" :en))
;; ⇒ 2
```

`string-append` (SRFI-13), `random-integer` (SRFI-27), `vector-ref` and `cond` (core) — all present, none imported. Each helper has one job; the program folds them together. That is the shape most Motoi programs take: small `define`s calling library verbs that are already loaded.

## 1.10 What you should be able to do after this chapter

After reading chapter 1, you should be able to:

1. **Call a library verb** — a string, list, or random verb — with no import line.
2. **Explain why Motoi has no `import`** — the libraries are loaded at boot.
3. **Recognize `module`/`import`/`export`** as other dialects' words, not Motoi's.
4. **Name Motoi's standard libraries** (R7RS-small + SRFI-1/13/125/27).
5. **Use `define`** to make and name your own helpers instead of an `export` list.
6. **Compose a few small helpers** into one program, the way section 1.9 shows.

If any of those six feel wobbly, reread the section that covers them before moving on.

## 1.11 What Cortex sees

One real Motoi capability before you turn the page: Cortex. Motoi programs can record and recall facts, and query them back. This *is* a Motoi feature (unlike `module`), so here it is running:

```scheme
(cortex/write 'greeting-count 3)
(cortex/recall 'greeting-count)
```

`cortex/write` stores a fact; `cortex/recall` reads it back. Chapter 13 covers Cortex in full. I mention it here only so the diagram in your head from day one includes the observer.

The core is small. The libraries are loaded. Cortex is watching. That is Motoi.

Turn the page.")
